We will use a lightweight relational database for our persistence store.  The tables are on the order of a few thousand rows each (maximum). 

### Requestors  
* Requestor\_ID (integer).  primary key.  Auto-generated random number.  Unique.  
* Email (string).  Required.  
* first_name (string). May be null.
* last_name (string).  May be null.
* address (string)
* city (string)
* state (string).  US state.
* zip (string).  ZIP code.
* Phone (string).  Backup communication method.   May be null.  
* Credits (integer).  Required.  Users with more credits are higher priority and will get to choose first.  
* login_code (integer).  May be null if never logged in.
* code_generated_when (Datetime).  may be null.  For code expiry, when did we send their email code most recently.  
* Admin (boolean).  Does this person have admin rights.  
* Creation\_date. (datetime).  When was the user record created.  
* Last\_mod\_date. (datetime).  When was this user record last edited.
* last\_failed\_login (datetime).  When was the last bad login code entered (for limiting brute force attacks)
* years\_of\_service.  250-character text field with a concatenation of all the years  the requestor has volunteered.
* has_a_chainsaw  (boolean)
* chainsaw_user (boolean)
* other_skills (250-character text field).  Other skills that the user wants to highlight.
* private comments (250-character text field).  Comments that work party leaders can leave about a volunteer, not to be seen by that volunteer.
* liability_waiver_date (datetime).  When was the most recent and properly executed liability waiver received for this volunteer.
* lottery_value (integer, nullable).  Random number assigned to this requestor which may be used as a tie-breaker.  This is assigned separately from the assignment algorithm, may remain empty until first regeneration, and does not change the requestor's identity or other profile fields.

### Work Parties
This table can grow year over year so that we record all the work parties hosted over time.
* Friday evening check-in (date).  Primary key.
* Hut (string).  Primary key.
* Sunday evening check-out (date)
* Leader (string)
* Leader phone (string)
* Capacity (integer)
* Party comments (string).  Hiking distance, elevation, other things of note.

### work party requests
This is a table to capture the intersection of work parties and requestors.  This table grows over the years as requestors participate in more work parties.
* Friday evening check-in (date).  Primary key.
* Hut (string).  Primary key.
* Requestor\_ID (integer).  Primary key.
* Interest (string).  One of "no thank you", "only if you need me", and "please consider".  We don't need to store records recording "no thank you" -- a missing intersection record can represent this status.
* Confirmation status (string).  One of "Pending", "Waitlisted", "Accepted".  This field can be blank, which is the same as if the intersection record is not present (use an outer join to produce this blank value in a join between requestors and work parties if necessary)
* Attendance status (string).  This is a sub-status field of confirmation to document any details of what happened in the work party.  Can be blank, or one of "Cancelled", "No-show", "full attended", or "partial attended".
* added date (datetime).  When the requestor selected the work party, can be used for waitlist management.

### ski trip requests
  * Request\_ID (integer).  Primary key.
  * Requestor\_ID (Integer).  
  * Benson.  Boolean.  
  * Bradley.  Boolean.  
  * Grubb.  Boolean  
  * Ludlow.  Boolean.  
  * Arrival (date).  Check-in date.   For combination trips, the second arrival should be the same as the first departure.
  * Departure (date).  Ending date (check-out) of the hut stay.  Must be after Arrival.  
  * Choice\_Number (integer).  For example, 1 \= first choice, 2 \= second choice, etc.  A combination trip will have the same choice number twice with contiguous (non-overlapping) dates, for example a first choice with Benson and another first choice with Bradley, and the arrival date for the second request matches the departure date for the first request.
  * Spots\_ideal.  Number of people requested.  Required to be between 1 and 15 (12 max for some huts).    
  * Spots\_min.  The fewest \# of spots the requestor would accept without going to their next choice.  
  * Hut\_granted.  Which hut was selected of the options.  
  * Spots\_granted.  Spots that were available for accepted requests.  
  * Status. (string).  requested, granted, lost-lottery, not-used.  Default is "requested".  (Legacy values pending/confirmed should be treated as requested/granted.)
  * Creation\_date. (datetime).  When was the request created.  
  * Last\_mod\_date. (datetime).  When was this request last edited.
  * hut_count_flexibility (integer).  How many huts the request is open to.  This is a calculated field, based on the # of huts marked TRUE.
  * saturday_week_number (integer).  This is a calculated field.  Using the saturday closest to the midpoint of the trip, which saturday does the trip cover.  This is calculated from the (departure date - arrival date) / 2 to get the midpoint, then find the closest saturday to that midpoint and calculate the week number for that saturday.
  * Combination_first_request.  (integer).  The Request\_ID of the first hut.
